{{smaller block|“Follow my white plume,” said the chivalrous monarch of France, as he plunged into the thickest of the ''vulgar'' fight.
{{right|''Sumner'', Orations, I. 188.}}}}
'''2.''' Common; in general use; customary; usual; ordinary.
{{smaller block|Our intent is to make this Art [Poesie] ''vulgar'' for all English mens vse.
{{right|''Puttenham'', Arte of Eng. Poesie, p. 19.}}
As naked as the ''vulgar'' air.
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, ii. 1. 387.}}
They have applied the sense of the parables to certain general and ''vulgar'' matters, without reaching to their real purport.
{{right|''Bacon'', Physical Fables, p. 8.}}
I shall much rejoice to see and serve you, whom I honour with no ''vulgar'' Affection.
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, I. ii. 24.}}
Unspeakable mysteries in the Scriptures are often delivered in a ''vulgar'' and illustrative way.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Religio Medici, i. 45.}}
If Wordsworth sometimes puts the trumpet to his lips, yet he lays it aside soon and willingly for his appropriate instrument, the pastoral reed. And it is not one that grew by any ''vulgar'' stream, but that which Apollo breathed through, tending the flocks of Admetus.
{{right|''Lowell'', Among my Books, 2d ser., p. 241.}}}}
'''3.''' Hence, national; vernacular: as, the ''vulgar'' tongue; the ''vulgar'' version of the Scriptures; in ''zoöl.'' and ''bot.'', specifically, vernacular or trivial, as opposed to ''scientific'' or ''technical'', in the names or naming of plants and animals. See ''pseudonym'', 2.
{{smaller block|If againe Art be but a certaine order of rules prescribed by reason, and gathered by experience, why should not Poesie be a ''vulgar'' Art with vs as well as with the Greeks and Latines?
{{right|''Puttenham'', Arte of Eng. Poesie, p. 3.}}
We will in this present chapter & by our own idle obseruations shew how one may easily and commodiously lead all those feete of the auncients into our ''vulgar'' langage.
{{right|''Puttenham'', Arte of Eng. Poesie, p. 86.}}}}

